se hot? Let’s see:\n\nStatement 14: No round people are green. Statement 15: No young, green people are round. These are about people, not animals, so probably relevant here.\n\nStatement 16 again: If someone avoids the mouse and the mouse ignores the lion, then they are not green. But the mouse is the one ignoring the lion, so unless someone else avoids the mouse, this affects the mouse\'s temperature.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the mouse ignores the lion (statement 4), which according to statement 11 means the mouse is hot. Therefore, the statement "The mouse is hot" would be true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Is there any way the mouse is hot? For example, if the mouse is not green, does that affect anything? Statement 16 says if someone avoids the mouse and the mouse ignores the lion, then they are not green. But the mouse is the one ignoring the lion, so unless someone else avoids the mouse, the mouse\'s color is directly determined. However, the tiger is not green (statement 6) and blue (statement 7), but that\'s about the tiger, not the mouse.\n\nAnother angle: Statement 13 says if no one avoids the tiger and the tiger ignores the cow, then the tiger avoids the lion. The tiger does ignore the cow (statement 5), so if no one avoids the tiger, then the tiger avoids the lion. But the tiger already avoids the lion (statement 8), so this might add something new.\n\nI think the key point is statement 4 and 11. The mouse ignores the lion, so it’s hot.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]  3%|2         | 16/600 [00:07<03:23,  2.86it/s]  3%|2         | 17/600 [00:07<03:02,  3.19it/s]
