lion.\n13.If something needs the lion, then it chases the squirrel.\n14.If the squirrel likes the cow, then the cow does not need the rabbit.\n15.If the squirrel likes the cow and the squirrel is not green, then the squirrel needs the rabbit.\n16.If something needs the squirrel, then the squirrel needs the cow.\n\nWait, but the question is about the rabbit being not rough.Looking at statement 5, it directly says "The rabbit is not rough."So that seems straightforward.But maybe I should check if there are any contradictions or dependencies that could affect this.\n\nLet me list out the roughness:.\n\n- Cow: rough (statement 1).\n- Lion: info on roughness, but it\'s not green (statement 2).\n- Rabbit: rough (statement 5).\n- Squirrel: rough (statement 7).\n\nSo according to the given statements, the rabbit is explicitly stated as rough. So the statement "The rabbit is rough" is false.\n\nBut wait, maybe I should check if any other statements could imply something else about the rabbit\'s roughness. Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If the squirrel dislikes the cow, then the cow needs the rabbit. But the cow\'s need for the rabbit directly affects the rabbit\'s roughness.\n\nStatement 15: If the squirrel dislikes the cow and the squirrel is green, then the squirrel does not need the rabbit. But the squirrel\'s color is mentioned, except that the lion is not green. So the squirrel could be not green or green, but that does affect the rabbit\'s roughness.\n\nStatement 16: If something does not need the squirrel, then the squirrel does not need the cow. Again, this relates to roughness.\n\nSo, all the other statements seem to affect the rabbit\'s roughness. The only statement that directly mentions the rabbit\'s roughness is statement 5, which says it\'s rough.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
