rry should not be blue.\n\nWait, but let\'s check if there\'s any agreement. Statement 10 says if something is red and foolish, it\'s blue. But we do have some information that Harry is red, so this might affect Harry directly.\n\nMoving on, statement 14 says if Harry is foolish and not blue, then he is not red. We\'ve established Harry is foolish and, from statement 9, he\'s not blue. So, applying statement 14, Harry must not be red.\n\nNow, statement 13 states that not red things are not furry. Since Harry is not red, this would mean Harry is not furry.\n\nLet me double-check to make sure I didn\'t miss anything. Harry is foolish (7), so he\'s cruel (8). Cruel things are loud (11), so Harry is loud. Loud and foolish things are not blue (9), so Harry is not blue. Since Harry is foolish and not blue, he must not be red (14). And not red things are not furry (13), so Harry is not furry.\n\nI do see several contradictions in this chain of reasoning. Each step illogically contradicts the previous one based on the given statements.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
