ce all the given statements are true, this can\'t be the case.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the tiger is not big, which would mean that statement 15 is false, but that can\'t be because the tiger chases the lion, so statement 15 says it must be big.\n\nThis is a bit of a loop.Let me try to see if there\'s another way.\n\nWait, maybe the tiger is big, but it doesn\'t visit the dog because of another rule.Let me check statement 19: If the tiger visits the dog and the dog is not big, then the dog chases the mouse.But the dog is nice (statement 2), and the mouse is nice (statement 7).There\'s no mention of the dog\'s size, but the lion is not big (statement 4).The dog chases the lion (statement 1), so according to statement 15, the dog would be big.Wait, does the dog chase the lion?Yes, statement 1 says the dog chases the lion.So, according to statement 15, the dog is big.\n\nWait, that\'s a new point.The dog chases the lion, so according to statement 15, the dog is big.So, the dog is big.\n\nNow, statement 17: If something is small, it ignores the cat. So, the tiger is small (from fleeing the mouse), so it should ignore the cat. But in the given context, the tiger does visit the cat. Therefore, this is a confirmation.\n\nWait, but the cat is small, so according to statement 17, the tiger (being small) should ignore the cat. But the tiger does, so that\'s not a problem.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger is big, which would mean that statement 15 is true, but that can be because the tiger runs from the mouse, so it must be small.\n\nThis is really clear. Let me try to outline the implications:\n\n1. Tiger runs from mouse → Tiger is small (statement 15).\n2. Tiger is small → Tiger ignores cat (statement 17).\n3. But in the context, Tiger does visit cat → Confirmation.\n\nTherefore, the only way to resolve this is if the tiger is big, which would mean that statement 15 is true. But that can be because the tiger does run from the mouse, so statement 15 says it must be small.\n