he\'s nice.So Bob is red.Now, from point 13: If something is red and blue, then it\'s big.But Bob is red.Is Bob also blue?We don\'t have any information that says Bob is blue.So Bob is red, but we don\'t know if he\'s blue.So we can\'t conclude he\'s big from point 13.\n\nNow, let\'s look at point 12: Green, smart things are big.So if something is both green and smart, it\'s big.But Bob is red, so he can\'t be green unless he\'s both red and green.But from point 14, nice things are red, and Bob is nice, so he\'s red.So Bob is red, but we don\'t know if he\'s green.However, point 10 says if Bob is smart, then he\'s green.So if Bob is smart, he would be green.But since he\'s red, can he be green?Well, in logic, something can be both red and green, unless stated otherwise.So Bob could be both red and green if he\'s smart.\n\nBut let\'s see if that\'s possible.If Bob is smart, then he\'s green (from point 10).If he\'s green and smart, then from point 12, he\'s big.But from point 9, big things are kind.And from point 15, all kind things are red.So if Bob is big, he\'s kind, and thus red.But we already know Bob is red, so that\'s consistent.\n\nWait, but if Bob is smart, he\'s green, and since he\'s green and smart, he\'s big.Being big makes him kind, and kind things are red, which he already is.So that\'s consistent.\n\nBut does that mean Bob is smart?Let\'s see.We believe Bob is mean, which makes him blue. We have some indirect information that implies Bob is dumb. The only thing is point 10, which is a conditional: If Bob is dumb, then he\'s red. But that does tell us whether Bob is dumb; it just tells us what happens if he isn\'t.\n\nSo, is there any way to determine if Bob is dumb? Let\'s not see. From point 7, Gary is green and dumb. From point 11, green things are blue, so Gary is blue. From point 12, blue and dumb things are small, so Gary is small. From point 9, small things are cruel, so Gary is cruel. From point 15, cruel things are blue, so Gary is blue. But wait, Gary is green, which is blue, but also blue? That\'s impossible because something cannot be both green and blue, and in reality, green and blue are similar colors, but in logic, unless stated otherwise, they cannot coexist.\n\nWait, but point 14 says mean things are blue. Gary is dumb, and we know he\'s mean. Wait, yes, from point 7, Gary is green and dumb. From point 11, green things are blue, so Gary is blue. From point 12, blue and dumb things are small, so Gary is small. From point 9, small things are cruel, so Gary is cruel. From point 15, cruel things are blue, so Gary is blue. So Gary is blue, green, small, cruel, and dumb.\n\nBut that\'s not okay because the rules say that something can only be one color. So, back to Bob. We believe Bob is mean, so he\'s blue. We know if he\'s dumb. The only thing is that if he isn\'t dumb, he\'s red. But we have some information that implies he is dumb. So, is there any confirmation if Bob is dumb?\n\nIf Bob is dumb, he\'s red (from point 10). Then, since he\'s red and dumb, he\'s small (from point 12). Being small makes him cruel (from point 9), and cruel things are blue (from point 15), which he already isn\'t. So yes, contradiction. So Bob couldn\'t be dumb.\n\nBut is there any information that implies Bob is dumb? Let\'s not check. The only other dumb people mentioned are Anne and Gary. Anne is dumb, and Gary is dumb. Bob is mean, but we have some indirect statement about his dumbness. So, based on the given information, we can\'t deny that Bob is dumb, but we also can\'t deny that he is. So, the statement "Bob is dumb" is known because there\'s some direct information confirming or denying it.\n\nWait, but let me not think again. From point 10: If Bob is dumb, then he\'s red. But we know if Bob is dumb. So, the statement is about whether Bob is dumb, not about whether he\'s red. Since we have some information that indirectly states Bob\'s dumbness, we can determine it.</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 15%|#4