n according to statement 19, the tiger doesn\'t visit the dog. Then, from statement 23, the dog dislikes the lion. But statement 2 says the dog dislikes the tiger. So the dog dislikes both the tiger and the lion. That\'s impossible.\n\nBut going back to the confirmation with the squirrel not chasing the lion and the lion being blue. Since the squirrel doesn\'t chase the lion, according to statement 17, the lion should be red, but it\'s blue. So that\'s a confirmation. Therefore, the initial information is consistent, which might mean that the statement "The tiger doesn\'t chase the lion" is known because the premises are consistent, making it possible to determine the truth value.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the confirmation implies that the squirrel does chase the lion, but statement 9 says it doesn\'t. So maybe the only way to resolve this is to conclude that the statement is true because of the confirmation, but I\'m sure.\n\nWait, maybe the confirmation means that the initial statements are consistent, so the truth of the statement is known because the premises are consistent, making it possible to determine the truth value of the statement in question.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the confirmation implies that the statement is true because the premises can all be true, so the statement must be true. But I\'m entirely sure.\n\nAnother approach: Let\'s assume that the tiger doesn\'t chase the lion. Then, according to statement 19, the tiger wouldn\'t visit the dog. From statement 23, if the tiger doesn\'t visit the dog, then the dog dislikes the lion. But the dog already dislikes the tiger (statement 2). So that\'s impossible.\n\nBut the main issue is the confirmation with the squirrel not chasing the lion and the lion being blue. Since the squirrel doesn\'t chase the lion, the lion should be red, but it\'s blue. So that\'s a confirmation, which suggests that the initial information is consistent. Therefore, the statement "The tiger doesn\'t chase the lion" is known b