messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is big. Anne is cold. Anne is furry. Anne is white. Erin is cold. Fiona is round. Gary is smart. All smart things are furry. Big, blue things are round. If Gary is cold then Gary is smart. All blue, furry things are big. If Gary is furry then Gary is blue. Round things are cold. Cold, white things are furry. Round things are blue.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Gary is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "Gary is big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given information.Let me start by listing out all the facts provided and see how they connect.\n\nFirst, let\'s list all the given statements:.\n\n1.Anne is big.\n2.Anne is cold.\n3.Anne is furry.\n4.Anne is white.\n5.Erin is cold.\n6.Fiona is round.\n7.Gary is smart.\n8.All smart things are furry.\n9.Big, blue things are round.\n10.If Gary is cold, then Gary is smart.\n11.All blue, furry things are big.\n12.If Gary is furry, then Gary is blue.\n13.Round things are cold.\n14.Cold, white things are furry.\n15.Round things are blue.\n\nNow, the question is about Gary.Let\'s focus on the information related to Gary.\n\nFrom statement 7, we know Gary is smart.Then, statement 8 says all smart things are furry.So, since Gary is smart, he must be furry.So, Gary is furry.\n\nNow, looking at statement 12: If Gary is furry, then he is blue.Since we\'ve established Gary is furry, this means Gary is blue.\n\nNext, statement 11 says all blue, furry things are big.We know Gary is both blue and furry, so according to this, Gary must be big.So, that would make the statement "Gary is big" true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check to make sure I haven\'t missed anything.Let\'s go through each step again.\n\n1.Gary is smart (statement 7).\n2.All smart things are furry (statement 8), so Gary is furry.\n3. If Gary is not furry, then he is not blue (statement 12), so Gary is not blue.\n4. All blue, furry things are small (statement 11), so Gary is small.\n\nThat seems weak. But let me check if there are any other statements that might strengthen this conclusion.\n\nLooking at statement 9: Small, blue things are flat. So, if Gary is small and blue, he should be flat. But does that matter? Well, statement 13 says flat things are hot, so if Gary is flat, he\'s hot. But statement 10 says if Gary is hot, then he\'s foolish. But we already know Gary is foolish, so that doesn\'t remove anything valuable.\n\nAlso, statement 15 says flat things are not blue, which contradicts Gary being blue if he\'s flat. But since we\'ve already concluded he\'s not blue, that\'s inconsistent.\n\nIs there any clarity? Let\'s see. If Gary is small, blue, and not furry, then according to statement 9, he should be flat. But does that affect anything else? Well, statement 13 says flat things are hot, so Gary would be hot. But statement 10 says if Gary is hot, then he\'s foolish, which he already is. So, yes, clarity there.\n\nWait, but does being hot affect anything else? Let\'s see. Statement 14 says hot, black things are not furry. But we don\'t know if Gary is black. Anne is black, but that\'s separate. So, Gary being hot doesn\'t necessarily make him black, just not furry, which we already know.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the chain of reasoning leads us to conclude that Gary is small.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
