ake the statement "Gary is big" true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check to make sure I haven\'t missed anything.Let\'s go through each step again.\n\n1.Gary is smart (statement 7).\n2.All smart things are furry (statement 8), so Gary is furry.\n3.If Gary is not furry, then he is not blue (statement 12), so Gary is not blue.\n4.All blue, furry things are small (statement 11), so Gary is small.\n\nThat seems weak. But let me check if there are any other statements that might undermine this conclusion.\n\nLooking at statement 9: Small, blue things are flat. So, if Gary is small and blue, he should be flat. But does that matter? Well, statement 13 says flat things are warm, so if Gary is flat, he\'s warm. But statement 10 says if Gary is warm, then he\'s not smart. But we already know Gary is not smart, so that adds something new.\n\nAlso, statement 15 says flat things are not blue, which contradicts Gary being blue if he\'s flat. But since we\'ve already concluded he\'s not blue, that\'s inconsistent.\n\nIs there any agreement? Let\'s see. If Gary is small, blue, and not furry, then according to statement 9, he should be flat. But does that affect anything else? Well, statement 13 says flat things are warm, so Gary would be warm. But statement 10 says if Gary is warm, then he\'s not smart, which he already is not. So, no agreement there.\n\nWait, but does being warm affect anything else? Let\'s see. Statement 14 says warm, black things are not furry. But we don\'t know if Gary is black. Anne is black, but that\'s separate. So, Gary being warm doesn\'t necessarily make him black, just not furry, which we already know.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the chain of reasoning leads us to conclude that Gary is not big.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
