n the rabbit is young.The cat is rough (fact 2), but we don\'t know if the cat likes the rabbit.If the cat did like the rabbit, then the rabbit would be young.But the rabbit is already young?Wait, fact 3 says the cat is young, but the rabbit\'s age isn\'t directly stated.However, fact 13 says the rabbit is rough, and fact 24 might imply the rabbit is young if certain conditions are met, but I\'m not sure.\n\nWait, fact 3 says the cat is young, but the rabbit\'s age isn\'t directly given.However, fact 24 says if the rabbit chases the cat and the cat needs the lion, then the rabbit is young.But the rabbit chases the lion, not the cat, so this condition isn\'t met.So we can\'t conclude the rabbit\'s age from this.\n\nFact 17: If someone chases the lion, they are round.The cat chases the lion (fact 1), so the cat is round.\n\nFact 19: If someone is round and chases the lion, they need the cat.The cat is round (from fact 17) and chases the lion, so according to fact 19, the cat needs the cat.That seems a bit odd, but perhaps it\'s a way to say the cat needs itself, which might not be relevant here.\n\nFact 21: If someone chases the lion and the lion is blue, then the lion is round.The cat chases the lion (fact 1), and the lion is blue (fact 9).So according to fact 21, the lion is round.\n\nFact 25: If someone is round and they need the cat, then they chase the dog.The cat is round (from fact 17) and needs the cat (from fact 19, which seems a bit circular).So according to fact 25, the cat would chase the dog.But fact 1 says the cat chases the lion, not the dog.So this seems contradictory, but maybe it\'s a way to infer that the cat chases the dog as well?Or perhaps the conditions aren\'t fully met.\n\nWait, fact 25 says if someone is square and avoids the cat, then they ignore the dog. The cat is square (fact 17) and avoids the cat (from fact 19, which is a bit confusing because the cat avoids itself). So according to fact 25, the cat would ignore the dog. But fact 1 says