en.\n12.The tiger sees the bald eagle.\n\nNow, the conditional statements:.\n\n- If something is young and red, then it is green.\n- If something sees the bear, then it needs the tiger.\n- If something needs the bald eagle, then the bald eagle chases the rabbit.\n- If something is big and it chases the rabbit, then it sees the bear.\n- If something sees the rabbit and it needs the tiger, then the tiger is not blue.\n- If something chases the rabbit, then it sees the bald eagle.\n- If the rabbit is not young and the rabbit does not need the bald eagle, then the rabbit sees the bear.\n- If something is red and it needs the bear, then it is big.\n- If something is red, then it needs the bear.\n\nAlright, let\'s stop ignoring.\n\nFirst, the rabbit is not red (fact 8). From the conditional statements, if something is not red, then it does not need the bear (last statement). So, the rabbit does not need the bear.\n\nNow, looking at another conditional: If something is not red and it does not need the bear, then it is small. Since the rabbit is not red and does not need the bear, this condition applies. Therefore, the rabbit must be small.\n\nWait, but the question is whether the rabbit is big. If the rabbit is small, then the statement "The rabbit is big" would be false. So, the answer would be D) True.\n\nBut let me double-check to make sure I didn\'t add anything.\n\nLet\'s go through the steps again:\n\n1. Rabbit is not red (fact 8).\n2. Since it\'s not red, it does not need the bear (from the conditional: if not red, then does not need bear).\n3. Now, since it\'s not red and does not need the bear, another conditional says it must be small (if not red and does not need bear, then small).\n4. Therefore, the rabbit is small.\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
