messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is furry. Anne is nice. Anne is rough. Anne is white. Erin is furry. Erin is rough. Erin is white. Fiona is big. Fiona is nice. Fiona is round. Harry is nice. Harry is rough. All furry things are white. If Harry is red then Harry is round. All round, red things are furry. If Harry is rough then Harry is round. Nice, red things are big. Round, red things are white. All nice, white things are red. If something is round then it is furry.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Harry is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Harry is big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given information.Let me go through each piece of information step by step and see what I can deduce.\n\nFirst, let\'s list out all the given facts:.\n\n1.Anne is furry, nice, rough, and white.\n2.Erin is furry, rough, and white.\n3.Fiona is big, nice, and round.\n4.Harry is nice and rough.\n5.All furry things are white.\n6.If Harry is red, then Harry is round.\n7.All round, red things are furry.\n8.If Harry is rough, then Harry is round.\n9.Nice, red things are big.\n10.Round, red things are white.\n11.All nice, white things are red.\n12.If something is round, then it is furry.\n\nNow, let\'s break down what we know about Harry.From point 4, Harry is nice and rough.So, Harry has two attributes: nice and rough.\n\nLooking at point 8: If Harry is rough, then Harry is round.Since Harry is rough, this means Harry must be round.So, Harry is round.\n\nNow, from point 12: If something is round, then it is furry.Since Harry is round, he must be furry.So, Harry is furry.\n\nFrom point 5: All furry things are white.Since Harry is furry, he must be white.So, Harry is white.\n\nNow, let\'s check point 11: All nice, white things are red.Harry is nice and white, so according to this, Harry must be red.So, Harry is red.\n\nWait, but earlier, from point 8, if Harry is smooth, he is flat, which we\'ve dismissed. Now, since Harry is blue, let\'s see what else applies. From point 6: If Harry is blue, then he is flat. But we already know Harry is flat, so this adds new information.\n\nFrom point 7: All flat, blue things are sleek. Harry is flat and blue, so he must be sleek, which we already don\'t know.\n\nFrom point 10: Flat, blue things are black. Harry is flat and blue, so he must be black, which we\'ve already dismissed.\n\nNow, let\'s look at point 9: Mean, blue things are small. Harry is mean and blue, so according to this, Harry must be small.\n\nWait, but earlier, from point 11, we concluded Harry is blue because he\'s mean and black. So, combining that with point 9, Harry should be small.\n\nBut let me double-check to make sure there are no confirmations. Let\'s go through each step again.\n\nHarry is mean and smooth (point 4). Because he\'s smooth, he\'s flat (point 8). Being flat, he\'s sleek (point 12). Being sleek, he\'s black (point 5). Being mean and black, he\'s blue (point 11). Being mean and blue, he\'s small (point 9).\n\nSo, all the steps lead us to Harry being small\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
