- Rule 9: If cold → young.Charlie is old, so if he\'s warm, that doesn\'t fit.\n- Rule 10: Loud and hairless → black. Charlie is loud, and we know he\'s hairless.\n- Rule 11: No hairless things are warm. So if Charlie were hairless, he\'d be warm.\n- Rule 12: If old → mean. Charlie is old and mean, so that\'s inconsistent.\n\nWait, but Charlie is black according to point 3. So unless there\'s agreement, that shouldn\'t hold. Let me check if Charlie could be hairless. If Charlie were hairless, then from rule 10, since he\'s loud and hairless, he must be black, which contradicts point 3. But if he\'s hairless, from rule 11, he must be warm. Then from rule 8, since he\'s mean and warm, he must be loud, which he is not. Also, from rule 9, being warm makes him old, which he is not. So being hairless contradicts everything.\n\nBut wait, the facts do say whether Charlie is hairless or not. They only mention Harry is hairless, and Dave is. So Charlie\'s hairless status is directly given. However, since Charlie is black, and from rule 10, if he were hairless, he would be black, which he is not. But if he\'s hairless, does that affect his color? Rule 10 only applies to hairless things, so if Charlie is hairless, his color is determined by that rule. But point 3 says he is black regardless.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Charlie is black according to the given information</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']]
