tatement 3 that the cat eats the bear, so the cat is alive and eating the bear.If someone were chasing the squirrel, they would eat the cat, but the cat is already eating the bear, so maybe that\'s a contradiction?Or perhaps the cat is being eaten by someone else, but that\'s not directly relevant to the squirrel chasing the bear.\n\nStatement 10: If the mouse eats the cat, then the cat chases the squirrel.But from statement 8, we know the cat eats the mouse because the bear likes the cat.So, the mouse is eaten by the cat, which means the condition in statement 10 isn\'t met (since the mouse isn\'t eating the cat).Therefore, statement 10 doesn\'t come into play here.\n\nStatement 11: If something eats the mouse, then the mouse chases the squirrel.We know the cat eats the mouse (from statement 8), so according to statement 11, the mouse chases the squirrel.So, the mouse is chasing the squirrel.\n\nWait, but statement 5 says the mouse does not chase the bear.So, the mouse is chasing the squirrel but not the bear.That\'s interesting.\n\nNow, let\'s think about statement 12 again: If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the cat.So, if the squirrel is being chased, it will eat the cat.But from statement 3, the cat is eating the bear, so the cat is alive.If the squirrel were to eat the cat, that would mean the cat is dead, but the cat is still eating the bear, so that can\'t happen.Therefore, the condition in statement 12 must not be met, meaning the squirrel is not being chased by anyone.Because if it were, the squirrel would eat the cat, which contradicts the cat eating the bear.\n\nWait, but from statement 11, the mouse is chasing the squirrel because the cat eats the mouse.So, the mouse is chasing the squirrel, which would trigger statement 12: if something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the cat.But the cat is eating the bear, so the squirrel can\'t eat the cat because the cat is alive.Therefore, this creates a contradiction unless the sq