meone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse.\n16.If someone likes the dog, then the dog visits the rabbit.\n17.If the mouse visits the lion and the mouse needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion.\n18.If the rabbit needs the lion, then the lion needs the mouse.\n19.If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the mouse does not need the dog.So, I need to determine if the mouse needs the dog or not.\n\nLooking at the facts, point 8 says the mouse does not need the rabbit.That\'s about the rabbit, not the dog.So, that doesn\'t directly help, but maybe other points do.\n\nPoint 9 says the mouse visits the dog.So, the mouse visits the dog.Now, looking at point 19: If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog.Wait, no, point 19 says if someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog.But in our case, the mouse is visiting the dog, not the other way around.So, does that trigger anything?Hmm, maybe not directly.\n\nWait, let me check point 19 again: "If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog."So, if someone else visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog.But in our case, the mouse is visiting the dog, so that\'s the opposite.So, maybe that doesn\'t apply here.\n\nLooking at point 17: If the mouse visits the lion and the mouse needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion.So, if both conditions are met (mouse visits lion and mouse needs dog), then the dog needs the lion.But we know from point 5 that the lion needs the dog.So, if the dog needs the lion, that would create a mutual dependency, but I\'m not sure if that\'s relevant yet.\n\nPoint 16: If someone likes the dog, then the dog visits the rabbit.The mouse likes the dog (point 7), so according to point 16, the dog should visit the rabbit.Does that happen?Let\'s see.Point 11 says the rabbit visits the dog, but nothing about the dog visiting the rabbit.So, maybe that\'s a point to consider.If the dog does visit the rabbit, that might have implications elsewhere.\n\nPoint 15: If someone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse.The rabbit visits the dog (point 11), but does the rabbit visit the mouse?Point 14 says if someone is round and likes the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the mouse.The lion is round (point 4) and does the lion like the rabbit?I don\'t think that\'s stated.The lion needs the dog (point 5), but nothing about liking the rabbit.So, maybe point 14 doesn\'t apply here.\n\nPoint 13: If someone needs the mouse, then they are kind.The mouse is big (point 6), but does anyone need the mouse?Point 18 says if the rabbit needs the lion, then the lion needs the mouse.So, if the rabbit needs the lion, then the lion needs the mouse.But does the rabbit need the lion?I don\'t see any information that the rabbit needs the lion.The rabbit visits the dog (point 11), but nothing about needing the lion.\n\nPoint 12: If someone needs the dog, then they visit the lion.The lion needs the dog (point 5), so according to point 12, the lion should visit the lion?Wait, that doesn\'t make sense.Wait, no, point 12 says if someone needs the dog, then they visit the lion.So, the lion needs the dog, so the lion should visit the lion?That seems a bit circular.Maybe I\'m misunderstanding.Alternatively, perhaps the lion needs the dog, so the lion would visit the lion, but that\'s the same entity, so maybe it\'s a typo or something else.Alternatively, maybe it\'s a way to say that the lion, needing the dog, would visit the lion, but that doesn\'t make much sense.Maybe it\'s a way to say that the lion, needing the dog, would visit the lion, but that\'s the same as itself, so perhaps it\'s a way to say that the lion would visit itself, which is a bit odd.Alternatively, maybe it\'s a way to say that the lion, needing the dog, would visit the lion, but that\'s the same as itself, so perhaps it\'s a way to say that the lion would visit the lion, which is redundant.Maybe I\'m overcomplicating this.\n\nLet me try to see if the mouse needs the dog.The question is whether the mouse does not need the dog.So, if we can find that the mouse does need the dog, then the statement is false.If we can find that the mouse does not need the dog, then the statement is true.If we can\'t determine, then it\'s unknown.\n\nFrom point 8, the mouse does not need the rabbit.So, the mouse\'s needs are not the rabbit.But does the mouse need the dog?Let\'s see.\n\nPoint 19: If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog.So, if someone else visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog.But in our case, the mouse is visiting the dog, not the other way around.So, does that mean anything?Maybe not directly.\n\nPoint 17: If the mouse visits the lion and the mouse needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion.So, if both conditions are true, then the dog needs the lion.But we know the lion needs the dog (point 5).So, if the dog ignores the lion, that would eliminate a mutual independence. But does the mouse avoid the lion? Point 9 says the mouse ignores the dog, but everything about avoiding the lion. So, unless the mouse also ignores the lion, point 17 does apply.\n\nWait, point 9 says the mouse ignores the dog. So, the mouse is ignoring the dog. Now, looking at point 16: If someone dislikes the dog, then the dog avoids the rabbit. The mouse dislikes the dog (point 7), so according to point 16, the dog must avoid the rabbit. But does the dog avoid the rabbit? Point 11 says the rabbit ignores the dog, but everything about the dog avoiding the rabbit. So, maybe the dog does avoid the rabbit, but it\'s explicitly stated. If the dog does avoid the rabbit, then according to point 15: If someone avoids the rabbit, then the rabbit ignores the mouse. So, if the dog avoids the rabbit, then the rabbit would ignore the mouse. But point 11 says the rabbit ignores the dog, not the mouse. So, maybe that\'s a chain: mouse dislikes dog → dog avoids rabbit → rabbit ignores mouse. But does that