definitely it doesn\'t see the rabbit at all.\n\nI\'m feeling quite clear now. Let me summarize:\n\n- The mouse avoids the rabbit (statement 4). Does that mean the mouse doesn\'t see the rabbit? Definitely, unless there\'s a rule that connects avoiding to not seeing.\n\n- The mouse is old and warm. If the mouse avoids the rabbit, it becomes mean (statement 17), then avoids the cat (statement 14). But does the mouse see the rabbit?\n\n- The cat avoids the rabbit (statement 18), so the cat is mean (statement 17). The cat is mean and old, so it avoids the cat (statement 14), which is necessary.\n\n- The mouse needs the horse (statement 7), and the horse is red (statement 3) and definitely old. If the horse is old, then it\'s yellow (statement 19). So the horse is neither red nor yellow, regardless of its age.\n\nWait, statement 19 says if the horse is old, then it\'s yellow. But statement 3 says the horse is red. So, if the horse is old, it\'s yellow, but it\'s also red. That seems consistent unless the horse can be neither red nor yellow, which is unlikely but very relevant here.\n\nBack to the mouse. The mouse avoids the rabbit, which is warm and old. The mouse is warm and old. The mouse needs the horse. The horse avoids the rabbit, and the rabbit needs the horse.\n\nI think the key here is whether the mouse avoids the rabbit. Since the mouse avoids the rabbit, it\'s unlikely that the mouse doesn\'t see the rabbit, but the rules don\'t explicitly state that avoiding implies not seeing. However, statement 14 says that if someone is mean and old, they avoid the rabbit. The mouse is old, but is it mean? To be mean, the mouse needs to avoid the rabbit and be warm (statement 17). Does the mouse see the rabbit?\n\nThe cat avoids the rabbit (statement 18), but the mouse does. So, the mouse does see the rabbit, so it is nice, so it does see the cat. But the mouse avoids the rabbit, so definitely it doesn\'t see the rabbit at all.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse does see th