it does avoid the lion (premise 4), but the lion\'s actions towards the rabbit are specified. If the lion avoids the rabbit, that would make the lion small (premise 8). But if the lion is small, then according to premise 11, it would avoid the lion, which is itself. That would mean the lion avoids itself, which is the statement we\'re evaluating.\n\nWait, that seems like a loop. If the lion is small, it avoids the lion (itself). But how does the lion become small? If the lion avoids the rabbit, then it\'s small. But if the lion is small, it avoids the lion. So perhaps the lion is small because it avoids the rabbit, which in turn makes it avoid itself.\n\nBut let\'s try to trace this step by step.\n\n1. The bald eagle is small because it avoids the rabbit (premise 8).\n2. Since the bald eagle is small, it must avoid the lion (premise 11).\n3. If the bald eagle avoids the lion, then according to premise 14, the lion avoids the rabbit.\n4. If the lion avoids the rabbit, then the lion is small (premise 8).\n5. Since the lion is small, it must avoid the lion (itself) according to premise 11.\n\nSo that would mean the lion avoids itself, making the statement false.\n\nBut wait, does the lion actually avoid the rabbit? Because premise 14 says if something avoids the lion, then the lion avoids the rabbit. So if the bald eagle avoids the lion, then the lion avoids the rabbit. But does the lion actually do that? It seems not, based on premise 14.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Bald eagle avoids rabbit → bald eagle is small (premise 8).\n- Bald eagle is small → bald eagle avoids lion (premise 11).\n- Bald eagle avoids lion → lion avoids rabbit (premise 14).\n- Lion avoids rabbit → lion is small (premise 8).\n- Lion is small → lion avoids lion (premise 11).\n\nTherefore, the lion avoids itself, making the statement false.\n\nBut wait, is there any clarity in this reasoning? Let me check again.\n\n- The lion avoids the rabbit (from premise 14 because the bald eagle avoids 