ng the lion.\n\nIs there any statement that says whether the bald eagle avoids the lion?I do see one.So, we know the bald eagle avoids the lion.\n\nBut statement 16 says if nothing chases the rabbit and visits the lion, then the lion needs the rabbit.Since the bald eagle does not chase the rabbit, if it visits the lion, then the lion needs the rabbit.But from statement 3, the lion does not need the rabbit.So, if the lion does not need the rabbit, then the condition in statement 16 must hold.That is, the bald eagle must not visit the lion.Because if it did, then the lion would need the rabbit, which supports statement 3.\n\nSo, the bald eagle must not visit the lion.Therefore, the condition in statement 16 is met, so the conclusion applies, and the lion does not need the rabbit.\n\nNow, going back, the bald eagle is small (from statement 8), so it does not chase the lion (statement 11).The lion does not chase the rabbit (statement 14).The rabbit does not need the bald eagle (statement 5) and the lion (statement 6).The rabbit chases the lion (statement 4).\n\nNow, does any of this imply whether the bald eagle chases itself?.\n\nI do see a rule that directly addresses an animal not chasing itself.The rules are about avoiding other animals or not needing them.So, unless there\'s a rule that says an animal must chase itself, or can chase itself, we can determine it.\n\nLooking through all the statements, some of them mention an animal not chasing itself.So, the information given provides some evidence against the bald eagle chasing itself\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 87%|########6 | 520/600 [12:04<00:25,  3.09it/s]
