t\'s a valid assumption.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog doesn\'t see itself, so the conditional doesn\'t apply, and thus we can\'t conclude the dog is big.\n\nLet me check other conditionals that might affect the dog\'s size.\n\nConditional 1: If someone is big, then they eat the dog.\n\nSo, if the dog is big, then the dog would eat itself, which doesn\'t make sense.Alternatively, if someone else is big, they would eat the dog.But we don\'t know if anyone else is big yet.\n\nConditional 4: If someone eats the dog, then the dog is nice.\n\nBut from the facts, the dog eats the bear and the lion (facts 7 and 8).So, does anyone eat the dog?The lion eats the cat (fact 11), but I don\'t see anyone eating the dog.So, perhaps no one eats the dog, meaning this conditional doesn\'t apply.\n\nConditional 2: If someone is cold, then they are nice.\n\nThe cat is cold (fact 5), so the cat is nice.The lion is cold (fact 12), so the lion is nice.\n\nConditional 6: If someone eats the bear and they are nice, then they are blue.\n\nThe dog eats the bear (fact 7) and is nice?Wait, the dog is blue (fact 9), but is the dog nice?Let\'s see.The bear is nice (fact 1).The cat is cold, so the cat is nice (from conditional 2).The lion is cold, so the lion is nice.The dog\'s niceness isn\'t directly stated, but let\'s see.\n\nIf the dog eats the bear and is nice, then the dog is blue.But the dog is already blue (fact 9), so that doesn\'t add new information.Alternatively, if the dog is nice, then from conditional 6, since the dog eats the bear and is nice, the dog must be blue, which it is.\n\nBut does the dog being nice affect anything else?Let\'s see.\n\nIf the dog is nice, then from conditional 1, if someone is big, they eat the dog.But if the dog is nice, does that mean someone else is big and eats the dog?But we don\'t have any information that someone eats the dog, so perhaps the dog isn\'t big because if it were, it would eat itself, which doesn\'t make sense.\n\nWait, that\'s a bit co