 lion avoids the rabbit, then the rabbit avoids the mouse.But we know if the lion avoids the rabbit, so we can apply this.\n\nIs there no other rule that could lead to the mouse avoiding itself?.\n\nLooking at statement 12: If something is unkind, it ignores the mouse.The mouse is necessarily unkind, so that applies.\n\nStatement 13: Tiger is orange and unkind, so it ignores the rabbit.Does affect the mouse.\n\nStatement 14: As discussed, can apply.\n\nStatement 15: If something is unkind and ignores the mouse, it ignores the lion.The lion is unkind and ignores the mouse, so it ignores the lion.Does affect the mouse.\n\nSo, from all this, I do see some rule that would cause the mouse to avoid itself.The only avoiding we know is mouse → lion, and potentially rabbit → mouse if statement 14 applies, but we have enough info for that.\n\nWait, but let\'s think again about statement 14.If the mouse avoids the lion (which it does), and if the lion avoids the rabbit, then the rabbit avoids the mouse.But we know if the lion avoids the rabbit.So, unless we can determine that the lion avoids the rabbit, we can conclude that the rabbit avoids the mouse.\n\nIs there any way to determine if the lion avoids the rabbit?.\n\nLooking at statement 15: If something is unkind and ignores the mouse, then it ignores the lion.The rabbit is unkind and ignores the mouse, so the rabbit ignores the lion.So, rabbit → ignores lion.\n\nBut does that mean the lion avoids the rabbit?Yes, not necessarily.Avoiding is a different action.Ignoring doesn\'t imply avoiding.\n\nSo, without knowing that the lion avoids the rabbit, we can apply statement 14.\n\nTherefore, the only avoiding we have is mouse → lion.There’s information that leads to the mouse avoiding itself\n</think>\nTherefore, the option is ']] 98%|#########8| 589/600 [12:36<00:08,  1.36it/s]
