ses the cat, which implies it sees the cat.So yes, the lion sees the cat.Therefore, according to this, the lion should be young.But the given info doesn\'t mention the lion\'s age, so perhaps the lion is young, but it\'s not stated.\n\n5.If something is cold, then it chases the cat.(Cold → Chases cat).\n   - The squirrel is cold (point 13), so according to this, the squirrel should chase the cat.Point 11 says the squirrel chases the lion, not the cat.Wait, that\'s a problem.The squirrel is cold, so it should chase the cat, but according to point 11, it chases the lion.So this seems contradictory.Maybe the squirrel avoids neither the lion nor the cat? Or perhaps the condition is that if something is hot, it avoids the cat, but the squirrel is hot and avoids the lion, which supports the condition. So perhaps the condition is met, meaning the squirrel is hot, but point 13 says it isn\'t. So this is not a contradiction.\n\n6. If the squirrel ignores the bald eagle and the bald eagle is warm, then the bald eagle is flat. (Squirrel ignores BE ∧ BE warm → BE flat).\n   - The squirrel ignores the lion (point 12), not the bald eagle. So the condition is met, so this does apply.\n\n7. If something avoids the bald eagle and it is old, then it avoids the squirrel. (Avoids BE ∧ Old → Avoids squirrel).\n   - The squirrel avoids the bald eagle (point 10). If the squirrel is old, then it should avoid the squirrel. Wait, that does make sense. Maybe it\'s another animal avoiding the bald eagle. The squirrel avoids the bald eagle, but if it\'s old, then it avoids the squirrel. But the squirrel is avoiding itself? That seems normal. Alternatively, maybe the lion avoids the bald eagle, but the lion is mentioned as avoiding the bald eagle. The squirrel avoids the bald eagle, so if the squirrel is old, then it avoids the squirrel. But the squirrel is unkind (point 14), not necessarily old. So perhaps this condition does apply.\n\n8. If something ignores the squirrel and it ignores the cat